ata is actually net  new so where is all this data coming  from well I mean part of it is from you  taking pictures uploading them videos  etc but a big part of it is actually  from health care and life sciences  medical imaging is actually number one  but genomics is not that far from it and  the amount of genomics day that is  actually exponentially growing and  resulting in a huge challenge in terms  of how we can handle it and manage this  data this growth means that there needs  to be like a new medium or way of  handling it and when is the end result  of this well we're already seeing a  profound impact on healthcare we've  barely scratched the surface of what  genomic research can actually yield but  it's already having a significant impact  in 2018 around one-third of new  fda-approved drugs actually relied on  genomics and interesting enough in 2005  Francis Collins the director of national  of the National Human Genome Research  Institute predicted that most drugs by  2020 would be derived or used using the  understanding of the human genome and  we're actually not that far from it and  another interesting fact is by this year  the expectation in 2020 the expectation  that we'll be around 26 million people  while shared their DNA with one of the  four leading commercial ancestry forms  so this is actually becoming a trend  another interesting trend is what we  call as a rise of genomic bio bags it's  quite interesting  while iBanking is sort of the result of  advances in human genome in computing  and bioinformatics and if you  think of it it's really a modern way of  storing managing and analyzing massive  amounts of biological data many  population-based bio banks have been  formed in different countries and is  actually becoming a central national  strategy some examples include the UK  biobank the Fijian project in Finland  and the biobank grass in Austria and why  banking is actually quite important  because they act as a central hub for  other organizations such as farmers  universities research institutes and  they provide a way for these Institute's  or organisations to access data to  conduct research there are strategic  very strategic because they help the  industry overall and format form as an  accelerator for research yet they still  face very common challenges so let's  talk a bit about that some because of  because sequencing has become a lot more  prevalent and cheaper  we're just generally getting a huge  increase in genomic data and this rapid  growth is really in petabytes of data  and that means when you're actually  needing to share that data among other  organizations you're talking about an  exabyte level of sharing this large  growth of data together with the  complexity of having to analyze a lot of  this data requires a new scale of  compute and storage and when we talking  new scale we're not talking a few  hundred or thousands VMs like in many  cases or that we've seen you need  something like a hundred thousand cores  to be able to do one process over from a  result of a sequencing activity most  organizations don't have that scale or  resource and in many cases they need to  be able to do it in a bursty way such  that they suddenly receive a large data  dump and they need to process it in a  very short period of time  what's happening because of this is that  technology is actually becoming the  limiter of research because many  organizations are not set up to be able  to handle this love  of compute so how does cloud actually  help solve this problem well if you  start by looking at the traditional way  in which things worked and when you see  a traditional we mean this is how  biobanks research institutes and farmers  all are set up to work today  this is the environment and process that  they have when the volumes of data are  small and the requirements were actually  quite different and how that works is  that all the data is centralized and in  a repository and whenever any researcher  or organization wants to conduct  research on that data they basically  make a copy and use it on their own  workstation or compute cluster where  effectively data sharing meant data  copying so if you think about it how is  this practical when you have been a  bicep data how do you actually manage to  process all that data if you're talking  about petabytes of data this requires  considerable compute and many of these  organizations have what effectively is  is fixed compute and they only Hardware  and they don't have the ability to scale  200,000 cores to be able to process that  data and with many scientists they would  have to wait in a queue because a  specific organization would only have  one compute cluster and for different  Sciences to be able to conduct work they  have to wait until the other ones are  done and another challenge that this  actually poses is security  implementation because the way that data  is shared each time it's shared in a  different way and copied that means you  need to put specific city a specific  custom security control mechanism for  each activity for each sharing of data  and that actually makes it quite  vulnerable because you're not you don't  have a comprehensive overarching  security methodology so how does public  cloud actually change this model well in  in public cloud all the data and compute  would be centralized and the researchers  and institutions would go to that data  add compute so they would only be  copy of the data maintained and how we  actually have access to that data will  be controlled is using things like I am  and identity access management where  each each organization or individual  that needs to access that data is  controlled the access is controlled at a  very granular level such that the only  access to the data they need and only  perform the actions they need to for the  benefit of the research and at the same  time because it's cloud-based it  provides an elastic level of compute and  sto