nly used  by by banks and research organizations  to process and analyze thousands of  whole genome sequences with ease but  working with common file formats for  genomics and variant data can be  challenging when working with machine  learning frameworks which was one of the  drivers of developing the variants  transform tool the variant transform  tool is an open-source tool developed by  Google cloud and now has many  contributors from the community it can  export variants data to bigquery this  makes it easy to interrogate the data  using SQL statements in a very fast and  efficient way it also makes it quite  easy to build datasets that can be  consumed easily by machine learning  frameworks or our AI platform to build  new machine learning models but  sometimes all the researchers want is to  simply access row compute power it could  be that they need a machine with more  than a hundred course or even terabytes  of memory to run something like a de  novo Gina sembly or use an HPC cluster  such as a slurm or any number of the  tools that they can run on top of Google  compute engine such as galaxy or toil  which can actually use the compute  engine API to provision instances many  of the bioinformaticians may also be  using research analysis platform such as  tera which was developed by verily which  is an alphabet company also by the Broad  Institute to enable researchers to  collaborate on workspaces share  notebooks and easily launch pipelines  through Cromwell integrations this makes  such analysis platforms a one-stop-shop  to access datasets and be able to run  your analysis and share that and  collaborate with others  and of course there are many more tools  from the community and our partner  ecosystems that can be added to this  toolbox for bioinformaticians so let us  look at Hale in a more bit of details as  I mentioned before Hale runs on top of  data block and data block enables you  not just to create a dupe cluster in  seconds but also enables yo