rage  pulling docker images and running that  step then the localizing the output  again to the cloud storage and it is  able to do that in scale the Broad  Institute for example have used the API  to call variants for all 79,000 whole  genome sequences in the nomad dataset in  just three days  several workflow engines or batch job  managers like d-sub taking advantage of  the Life Sciences API this includes  chromel as we said before which was  developed by the Broad next flow which  we will see later in the demo and snake  make which is a recent additions all  these workflow engine have very active  user community and with many standard  and sample pipelines in github that you  can easily run the life sciences API  makes it easy for the developers of  these workflow engines to build quickly  on top of Google Cloud and also include  tools that allows you to debug your  pipeline or generate data that can be  used to analyze your pipeline calculate  the compute cost of a pipeline or a  particular step in the pipeline on the  right hand side this is what a typical  pipeline might look like it's a pretty a  series of tasks that may include  scattering across multiple processes or  gathering and collating them for each  step the API will create a virtual  machine once the step is completed the  virtual machine is deleted this helps  keep the cost of the pipeline down as  you don't have to pay for a cluster and  standing all the time and you don't have  to pay for any idle instances now let us  run a sample pipeline on Google Cloud  using next flow this is a sample  configuration for a profile to run with  Google life's life sciences executor you  will notice I have specified the region  I want my compute instances to be and  the bucket used for temporary and  intermediate files I have also selected  which region the Life Sciences API will  sort the metadata about the execution of  the pipeline and finally I said the use  private addresses to true as I already  have an add gateway set up in the  project and I don't want to use public  IPS for added security now let's switch  to my virtual machine  so now I'm going to run a test pipeline  I wrote on my virtual machine to install  and run next flow I need to set some  environmental variables I've already  done that in my bash I see to save time  every time I'm using this so as you can  see I've added the the key to my service  account the version I want to run and  Google as an execution mode so now I'm  going to run the pipeline I'm going to  pass in a config file which has all my  configurations and project information  and I'm going to run my test pipeline  which just compares the performance of  GPU versus CPU  I'm going to select the Google life  sciences profile and I'm going to add  some options to actually be able to  debug and monitor the performance of my  pipeline so as you can see the pipeline  is now running it will automatically  create a virtual machine on Google Cloud  to see that on tower I can see that now  my workflow is running I can check on  the resources and the virtual machine  that it's using and I can actually  monitor deep while it's being executed  spoiler alerts the GPU beats the CPU in  this one I can also check previous  pipelines that I've ran before with  Tower I can check the CPU usage so I can  actually optimize my pipeline and select  the correct virtual machine shape I can  check memory execution time and so on  it's actually very helpful when  troubleshooting and actually optimizing  your pipelines for cloud so that was our  demo I hope you enjoyed it as much as I  did thank you very much for your time of  course there are many more tools out  there that are from our partners and  ecosystems and if you have any questions  please share them with us on the dory  thank you everyone for the opportunity  to present to you today it was very  exciting for us we look forward to  seeing your questions on the dory  thank you
 

